procstep.procdesc:
Control network development began with an assessment of all ~19,000 Cassini Imaging Science Subsystem (ISS) images [Porco et al., 2004] of Titan in the CB3 (938 nm) and MT1 filters with a pixel scale ranging from better than 100 meters per pixel to much less than 10 kilometers per pixel. All processing and network development was done using the USGS’ Integrated Software for Imagers and Spectrometers (ISIS) [https://isis.astrogeology.usgs.gov/9.0.0/]. Images were radiometrically calibrated using the standard Cassini ISS calibration (cisscal) with additional flat fields and filters applied to remove CCD artifacts, noisy pixels and cosmic rays not removed by calibration. The CB3 filter images were further processed through a multistep method utilizing the MT1 filter closest in time to reduce atmospheric effects and enhance feature visibility [Perry et al, 2005]. These steps resulted in ~11,000 CB3 images for network consideration. &#xD;
&#xD;

Before network development began, a preliminary update to image locations was applied to ~9,000 images (via ISIS’ deltack application) based on the previous efforts of co-author Karkoschka. Correspondences between overlapping images (tie points) were then identified using ISIS’ findfeatures application, which utilizes feature-based detector/extractor algorithms. Subsequent pixel and (where possible) sub-pixel matching was performed with ISIS’ area-based pointreg maximum correlation algorithm. When necessary, additional points were added manually to ensure that all images were well connected. The final network included 6,896 images having better than 5-kilometer resolution, 92,831 points and 4,591,931 measures. To tie the image control network to Titan’s geodetic coordinate system, ISS images were manually tied to an existing Cassini Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR, Elachi et al., 2004) mosaic (https://data.astro.cornell.edu/RADAR/) via 72 ground points, which were constrained during bundle adjustment. &#xD;
&#xD;

Bundle adjustment, a non-linear, least squares minimization of tie point location residuals, was performed using ISIS’ jigsaw application [Edmundson et al., 2012] to solve for point latitude, longitude, and radius and spacecraft position. Point positions were constrained to 4 km in latitude and longitude and 2 km in radius, and spacecraft location was constrained to 2 km. Camera angles were constrained to 0.5 degrees. Final bundle adjustment resulted in root mean square (RMS) tie point uncertainties of 583 m, 572 m, and 772 m, in latitude, longitude, and radius, respectively. This corresponds to RMS residuals of 0.83 pixels, with two-sigma (5th and 95th percentile) residuals of -1.15 and 1.17 pixels, and maximum residuals of 6.25 pixels. We therefore contend that the data is pixel-registered, rather than subpixel-registered. &#xD;
&#xD;

Mosaics were generated from a subset of 5,091 controlled images, which omits images that contain large feature-obscuring clouds or bright limbs, were low resolution (4.5 km and lower) or were simply too noisy. In addition to the basic and feature enhancing processing described above, images were weighted according to incidence angle (lower incidence preferred) and CCD position (to limit vignetting). All images were trimmed at an emission angle of 60 degrees, while most were trimmed at an incidence angle 70 degrees although some polar images were trimmed at an incidence angle of 80 degrees to maintain features where applicable and ensure adequate spatial coverage. We then applied a 3131-pixel high-pass filter to emphasize surface detail. The mosaics provided here are average mosaics such that the value of each pixel is the average value of all of the pixels at that location. The number of images at each pixel location typically ranged from 20 – 30 images in the leading and trailing hemispheres and south pole, to several hundred images in the sub and anti-jovian hemispheres and north pole. Because these are average mosaics, they should not be used for photometric analysis. Mosaics were produced in both equirectangular and polar (north and south) projection using Titan’s IAU-approved radius of 2,575 km [Archinal et al. 2018] at 702 m/pixel. We used ISIS’ ckwriter and spkwriter applications to create SPICE CK (camera pointing kernels) and SPK (spacecraft position kernels) for every image include in the control network. &#xD;
&#xD;

References: &#xD;
Perry, J., McEwen, A., Fussner, S., Turtle, E., West, R., Porco, C., Knowles, B., Dawson, D., & Cassini ISS Team (2005). Processing ISS images of Titan's Surface. Paper presented at the 36th Lunar and Planetary Science Conference, Lunar and Planetary Institute, Houston, TX. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/archive/nasa/casi.ntrs.nasa.gov/20050173947.pdf &#xD;
&#xD;

Edmundson, K. L., Cook, D. A., Thomas, O. H., Archinal, B. A., Kirk, R. L. 2012. Jigsaw: The ISIS3 bundle adjustment for extraterrestrial photogrammetry. International Annals of Photogrammetry, Remote Sensing, and Spatial Information Sciences, I-4, 203-208. Available as http://www.isprs-ann-photogramm-remote-sens-spatial-inf-sci.net/I-4/203/2012/isprsannals-I-4-203-2012.pdf. &#xD;
&#xD;

Elachi, C., et al., 2004. Radar: The Cassini Titan Radar Mapper. Space Sci. Rev. 115: 71-110, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-004-1438-9 &#xD;
&#xD;

Porco, C. C., et al., 2004. Cassini imaging science: Instrument characteristics and anticipated scientific investigations at Saturn. Space Sci. Rev. 115: 363-497, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-004-1456-7
